Hi Angus,

I would like to offer the released Solidon3D 0.3.3 for an independent,
hands-on modelling-software review. The practical question is simple: can a
beginner fix a dimensionally wrong downloaded STL without rebuilding it in a
full CAD package?

The test file has angled mounting holes in the wrong position. Solidon must
recognise them in the mesh, move them to the right place on their original
axes, and keep the change as a reversible history step. The point worth testing
is what happens to the feature's identity: the usual workaround of filling and
redrilling produces the same shape but a new feature, so any fit defined
against it is orphaned. Here the identity survives the move.

This is what the last releases made possible. A recognised hole, peg,
countersink or dome can now be moved, rotated, resized, duplicated and removed,
where before only the diameter could change. A panel shows the measured values
as editable fields with a live preview, and one option applies an edit to a
whole row of holes at once.

The entire review workflow is manual modelling software and does not require
the optional chat feature. Projects stay local, there is no subscription or
customer account, and the application runs on Windows, macOS and Linux.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/

I can provide the released build (the complete demo runs free until
30 October 2026; for a later test I will simply provide a licence key), the
deliberately flawed STL, expected measurements and comparison files. There is
no payment, no approval request and no expectation of a positive result.

